Z||000013||rockcrawl||01-22-2001||07:21 PM||||I've never had any type of headers on any of my Fieros, but as a general rule, with short, large diameter primaries, the torque peak will be at a higher RPM. With long, small diameter primaries, the torque peak will be at lower RPM. Unfortunately it seems like anyone making headers for the Fiero is just picking pipe sizes that <B>look</B> like they will make power instead of engineering them, but they sure do look nice. $800 seems to be a little steep for a set of headers, especially considering that the stock Fiero exhaust system has proven to be designed very well. Of course this is mostly just hearsay and opinions.  <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||01-22-2001||07:39 PM||||I kept the stock Camaro cast iron manifolds on my 3.4- They have larger ports and outlets than the stock Fiero manifolds, so I guess they are in between the originals and headers. With some creative exhaust work, you could use cheap 93-95 F-body headers. It would require a custom exhaust system, but should still be cheaper than $800 headers.||||reg||1
